fsck_ext4fs device-sector I/O regressions

run-regress-io builds the production io.c with strict pread/pwrite
substitutes.  They reject unaligned offsets and lengths, inject errors,
short transfers and EINTR, and verify buffer bounds and preservation of
neighboring bytes.  Sector sizes range from 512 through 8192 bytes.

run-regress-images checks disposable 4K-block ext4 images without root.
It exercises full fsck, journal replay, and an empty journal with
RECOVER still set.  It checks read-only behavior, surrounding superblock
bytes, repaired free counts, replayed data, clean flags, and e2fsck.

run-regress-sector-size requires root.  It runs the same fixtures
through raw virtual disks with 512, 1024, 2048 and 4096-byte sectors.
Recovery uses preen mode, as during boot.  No filesystem is mounted and
no existing disk or on-disk disklabel is modified.  vnd permits some
sub-sector requests, so the strict unit substitutes also remain needed.
vnd uses synchronous backing-file I/O and has no DIOCCACHESYNC ioctl.
The recovery cases also check that fsck accepts this unsupported
operation, as kernel recovery does, instead of failing after replay.

The default regress target includes all three, with the virtual-disk
target declared in REGRESS_ROOT_TARGETS.  regress/run-ext4fs.sh includes
this directory and builds in an executable temporary directory.
Only regression helpers are built there, not fsck_ext4fs.

To run just these tests with an executable object directory:

    obj=$(mktemp -d /tmp/fsck_ext4fs_io_obj.XXXXXXXX)
    MAKEOBJDIR="$obj" make -C /usr/src/regress/sbin/fsck_ext4fs_io

The scripts test sbin/fsck_ext4fs/obj/fsck_ext4fs without rebuilding or
installing it.  A missing or non-executable binary is an error, not a
request to build a replacement.  Override FSCK_EXT4FS to select another
existing binary.  KEEP_TMP=1 retains fixtures; failures always retain
them.  Unprivileged runs skip the device target.
